2.5 Probability boxes
Mathematically, a P-box is defined as a pair of lower and upper cumulative distribution
functions rF X, F Xs from the epistemic space Θ to [0,1] such that F Xpxq ď FXpxq ď
F Xpxq @ x P Ω and Ω is a classical event space, i.e. such that F X stochastically
dominates F X [49]. The upper and lower bounds for the CDFs are F X “ PpX ď xq
and F X “ PpX ď xq, respectively. A P-box can be viewed as a continuous form of
random sets and Dempster-Shafer structure. Note that the probability distribution
family associated with the random variable x can be either specified or not specified.
The former are generally named distributional P-boxes, or parametric P-boxes, the
latter are named distribution-free P-boxes, or non-parametric P-boxes [106].
