The main effect index reveals what is the importance of each uncertain input factor
on the uncertainty in the model output. It is relatively cheap to compute, especially
by employing given data methods, e.g. using input-output data retrived from a plain
Monte Carlo or by scatter plot decomposition [114]. The main drawback of the main
32

effect index is that it does not account for interactions between input factors (e.g.
inherently to the structure of the model M) nor it accounts for correlation between
inputs. Higher order Sobol’s effects (second and higher order interactions) compose the
so-called total effect index ST i. This is a variance-based measure of the influence of an
input i accounting for all the interactions with other uncertain factors. It is defined as
follows:
